{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The 180KB bundle everyone agreed to is now 340KB and nobody noticed</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Six months ago, the team wrote "keep the main bundle under 200KB" in a performance doc everyone nodded along to in a meeting. Nobody enforced it anywhere. Today it's 340KB, and no single PR looks like the one that broke it, because none of them did, on their own.
    </p>
    <p>
      A budget that only exists as a sentence in a document isn't a budget. It's a wish. The bundle grows one reasonable-looking dependency at a time until, one day, someone actually measures it and is shocked.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is the classic slow-leak failure mode: every individual PR that adds 3-8KB looks completely reasonable in review, and no reviewer has the full historical context to say "that's the fifteenth 5KB addition this quarter." The budget needed to be enforced at the only point where the full picture is visible, in CI, automatically, on every PR.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The deeper problem is that a documented budget with no automated check has zero cost to violate and zero signal when it happens, so it converges to "the largest bundle size nobody complained about yet" rather than to the number that was actually agreed on.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Make the budget a build step, not a policy</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Instead of trusting people to remember and check a number by hand, a tool like <code>size-limit</code> or <code>bundlesize</code> measures your actual built output in CI and fails the pull request if it's over the limit you configured. No PR merges without either staying under budget or someone deliberately raising it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The config lives in the repo, next to the code, reviewed the same way as everything else: <code>{ "path": "dist/main.js", "limit": "200 KB" }</code>. When a PR pushes past it, the CI check goes red with the exact before/after size in the PR itself, at the moment someone can still easily see which change caused it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This converts a social problem (remembering to check, or challenging someone else's PR on size) into a mechanical one (the CI check is red or it isn't). Raising the budget is still possible and sometimes correct, but it now requires a deliberate, visible diff to the budget file itself, not silence.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The performance doc nobody opened again</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team wrote a performance budget doc after a slow launch scared everyone into caring. It had real numbers: 200KB JS, 100KB CSS, 2.5s Time to Interactive on a mid-tier phone. It was linked from the README. Nobody opened it again until a customer complained about load time fourteen months later.
    </p>
    <p>
      By then the JS bundle was 410KB. Every individual PR along the way had passed code review just fine, because "does this look reasonable" and "does this keep us under budget" are different questions, and only the doc, which nobody was checking, was tracking the second one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix wasn't a new doc or a stricter review process, it was <code>size-limit</code> wired into CI with the same 200KB number, now enforced automatically. The first PR after it landed immediately failed, on a change that had already been approved and merged in review the week before, on a completely unrelated feature.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      They set the initial limit slightly above the current real size rather than the original aspirational 200KB, specifically so the gate would start green and force a deliberate, tracked reduction plan afterward, rather than immediately blocking all merges on day one with an already-blown budget.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

